[17592186341549 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186339519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341549 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "60dfcf4a-5fb8-5dc8-acbc-e1191345ca9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342137 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74041" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342137 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d,$ if $c\\cdot a=-2,$ $c\\cdot b=4,$ and $d\\cdot b=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8427d1de-81ba-51ab-8f40-aacdc88c9eba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342137 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d & =(3a)\\cdot c-(4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d \\\\  & =3(a\\cdot c)-4(b\\cdot c)+2(b\\cdot d).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(a\\cdot c)-4(b\\cdot c)+2(b\\cdot d) & =3(c\\cdot a)-4(c\\cdot b)+2(d\\cdot b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $c\\cdot a,$ $c\\cdot b$ and $d\\cdot b,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(c\\cdot a)-4(c\\cdot b)+2(d\\cdot b) & =3(-2)-4(4)+2(5) \\\\  & =-6-16+10 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2d)\\cdot b=-12.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342140 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342140 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342140 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342140 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $a\\cdot a=0$\n\nII. $a\\cdot b>0$\n\nIII. $a\\cdot b<0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342140 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "576bb373-56a3-5da0-ab43-2d02b000f274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342140 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $a\\ne 0$, we must have $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. Simplifying the expression $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot b & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $-a\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421874 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421874 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421874 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09065806-b133-53e7-a415-f60daab53b99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421875 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421875 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421875 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ff61a3d-11e1-5c24-a4c0-eb5c2f727280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421876 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{25}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421876 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421876 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "105fb53f-a973-5605-818d-1c2b476234cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421877 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421877 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421877 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "201f2230-7957-57ee-9f4c-64a64340bee2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421878 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421878 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421878 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "650f79ed-8cca-54b7-b878-11eaac10aaa0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421879 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421879 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421879 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "71b3b29c-1c8b-59df-b6b7-57376201da06" #edb/ref 17592186045417]