186421882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421885 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421885 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421885 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421885 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cb20dfa1-3b66-5467-a387-ca9ce4c4fd61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421886 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421886 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39207" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421886 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421886 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $(4a+2b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $a\\cdot a<0$\n\nII. $b\\cdot a=\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nIII. $b\\cdot a=-2(a\\cdot a)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421886 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34e8f50c-adbf-51d4-b0d4-a2150385ab67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421886 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $a\\ne 0,$ we must have $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false since it leads to a contradiction. If $b\\cdot a=\\frac{1}{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4a+2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 4(a\\cdot a)+2(b\\cdot a) & =0 \\\\ 4(a\\cdot a)+2(\\frac{1}{2}) & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a & =-\\frac{1}{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut this is a contradiction since $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Simplifying the expression $(4a+2b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4a+2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ (4a)\\cdot a+(2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 4(a\\cdot a)+2(b\\cdot a) & =0 \\\\ 2(b\\cdot a) & =-4(a\\cdot a) \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-2(a\\cdot a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421887 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421887 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74045" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421887 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421887 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${b}_{2}=q{b}_{1}+a$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2}=-4.$ If $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and ${b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$, find the value of $q.$ \n\n*Hint: You might wish to take the equation ${b}_{2}=q{b}_{1}+a$ and find the dot product of both sides with ${b}_{1}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421887 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5008b167-e8a9-508a-9bad-146a4857fbba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421887 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take the equation ${b}_{2}=q{b}_{1}+a$ and find the dot product of both sides with ${b}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & =q{b}_{1}+a \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & ={b}_{1}\\cdot (q{b}_{1}+a) \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & ={b}_{1}\\cdot (q{b}_{1})+({b}_{1}\\cdot a) \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & =q({b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})+({b}_{1}\\cdot a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the formula for the dot product to compute the values of ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1} & ={b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =9+16 \\\\  & =25 \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot a & ={b}_{1}{a}_{1}+{b}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3+8 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1},$ ${b}_{1}\\cdot a,$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & =q({b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})+({b}_{1}\\cdot a) \\\\ -4 & =q\\cdot 25+11 \\\\ -25q & =15 \\\\ q & =-\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
